window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-11-28T04:50:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-11-27","event":"Printed: Tokyo CPI (Nov): core 2.9% from 2.6% (consensus 3.0%), headline 2.8%, core-core 2.5%; liquidity-supply auction (5 to 11 years) cover 3.89, tail 0.1 bp; Nikkei 58,711.11 (+2.11%), TOPIX 3,815.20 (+1.73%), VI 28.91; JGBs 2-year 1.760%, 10-year 2.640%, 30-year 3.870%; USD/JPY 159.56 New York close; MoF investor meeting (no readout found); US half-session S&P 500 +1.60%, Nasdaq +2.31%","why":"The early CPI read moved no pricing, the equity rebound broadened, and the yen closed 44 sen from 160 on the month-end fix."},{"date":"2026-11-30","event":"Industrial production prelim (Oct, 08:50; ING and Longbridge +2.1% m/m, +2.7% y/y; MetaTrader +0.2% m/m; METI survey +5.1%); retail sales (Oct, 08:50; +2.7% y/y expected); Ueda speech in Nagoya (10:05) and press conference; 2-year JGB auction (¥2.8tn); housing starts (Oct, 14:00; −6.7% y/y expected); MoF intervention total (Nov, 19:00); China November PMIs","why":"Ueda's last scheduled words before the December meeting; the October hard data test the manufacturing and household legs; the intervention total shows whether the MoF acted near 160; the 2-year auction tests demand at 1.76%."},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Unemployment and jobs ratio (Oct, 08:30; September 2.4% and 1.19); MoF corporate survey (Q3, 08:50; capex expected +4.3% y/y); manufacturing PMI final (Nov; flash 53.7); Huang closed-door hearing before three House committees; US ISM manufacturing and JOLTS","why":"Confirms the manufacturing leg and the labour side of the wage story; the capex figure feeds the GDP second estimate; the Nvidia governance strand drives the Nikkei leaders."},{"date":"2026-12-02","event":"Consumer confidence (Nov, 14:00; October 35.0); monetary base (Nov); US ADP","why":"A print under 35 is the Slowdown trigger on the household leg."},{"date":"2026-12-03","event":"Services PMI final (Nov; flash 50.5); 10-year JGB auction (MoF calendar; previously listed 1 Dec); Huang before Senate Banking; US ISM services","why":"A services final under 50 moves the regime to Slowdown; the 10-year auction is the first December supply test after the 2.640% close."},{"date":"2026-12-04","event":"US November payrolls (consensus +90k, unemployment 4.1%, earnings +3.0% y/y) and Michigan sentiment","why":"The last US jobs print before the FOMC; a hot print widens the front-end gap that holds USD/JPY near 160."},{"date":"2026-12-08","event":"Q3 GDP second estimate (08:50; first −0.1% annualized); wages and real wages (Oct); household spending (Oct); 30-year JGB auction (MoF calendar); US FOMC starts (8–9 Dec, 97.4% for 25 bp)","why":"A second negative quarter on the revision is the Slowdown trigger; real wages are the virtuous-cycle test. The 30-year auction is the super-long demand test after the dealer readout that issuance stays flat."},{"date":"2026-12-10","event":"5-year JGB auction (MoF calendar); US CPI week begins","why":"The belly auction after the 2-year and 10-year set the December pace."},{"date":"2026-12-12","event":"Diet session ends; food consumption-tax cut bill due to pass the Upper House by this date","why":"About ¥5tn a year unfunded; the fiscal input to the super-long end."},{"date":"2026-12-15","event":"BoJ Tankan (Dec; September large manufacturers +24); machinery orders (Oct); liquidity-supply auction (5 to 11 years)","why":"The quarterly leading read three days before the BoJ meeting."},{"date":"2026-12-17","event":"20-year JGB auction (MoF calendar); BoJ meeting begins (17–18 Dec)","why":"Super-long supply on the eve of the decision."},{"date":"2026-12-18","event":"BoJ decision (pricing 74.7% for 1.50% per MogWatch, 27 Nov); national CPI (Nov, 08:30) the same morning","why":"The base case is a hike to 1.50%; the CPI prints hours before the statement."},{"date":"2026-12-24","event":"2-year JGB auction (MoF calendar); year-end FY2027 JGB issuance plan due around this date","why":"The plan is the super-long supply answer to the dealer and investor meetings."},{"date":"2027-01-05","event":"USTR Section 301 comments and hearing requests due (hearing from 25 Jan 2027; Japan 15% in total, alternative 20%)","why":"The window in which the 20% alternative tied to the investment commitments is argued; final action expected early 2027."}],"cb":{"label":"Tightening","note":"Held at 1.25% on 30 Oct by 8–1 with Takata dissenting for 1.50%. Tokyo November CPI (27 Nov): core 2.9% from 2.6%, the highest since July 2025 and a third month above 2%, against a 3.0% QUICK and Reuters consensus; headline 2.8%; core-core 2.5% from 2.6%; energy +9.5% as the subsidies ended; core m/m +0.5%. Tamura (Kofu, 26 Nov): the rate must reach neutral (about 2%) soon, upside price signs warrant accelerating the pace, 25 bp every few months is the base case and 50 bp \"may become necessary\", a hike to be discussed \"at the December or a later meeting\". Pricing: MogWatch OIS 74.7% for 18 Dec on trades to 21:06 JST on 27 Nov, unchanged on the day after the Tokyo CPI (73.5% at 13:07 JST per NERO's reading of the same model; 76.7% on 26 Nov, 84.7% on 20 Nov); implied 1.437% for December, 1.484% January, 1.595% March, average change +18.7 bp; 1.75% or higher by March 43.1%; a week earlier December was 1.459%. Pound Sterling Live put money markets at about 17 bp on 25 Nov; Goldman Sachs now expects three 25 bp hikes (December, March, June) to a 2.0% terminal rate; ANZ says the backdrop does not warrant aggressive tightening. JGBs closed 27 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, 15:28): 2-year 1.760% (+3 bp on QUICK's revised 1.730% for 26 Nov, the highest since 24 Nov), 5-year 2.065% (+1.5 bp), 10-year 2.640% (+1 bp; 2.625% morning low after the CPI), 20-year 3.560% (+0.5 bp), 30-year 3.870% (−2 bp), 40-year 4.090% (−2.5 bp); December futures 128.12 (−12 sen) in a 128.09 to 128.41 range; TONA December 98.5625 unchanged. The ¥500bn liquidity-supply auction (5 to 11 years) drew 3.89 cover (3.77) with a 0.1 bp tail, called somewhat strong; the front end and belly sold on the 2.11% equity gain, US 10-year yields near 4.92% in Tokyo hours and position-squaring before the 2-year auction on 30 Nov. MoF reference yields for 26 Nov: 2-year 1.747%, 10-year 2.704%, 30-year 3.956%, 40-year 4.049% (the 27 Nov row was not yet published). Under the framework the 2-year at 1.760% prices a December hike as the base case with part of a hold still in; the stance is Tightening with the pace disputed. The yen: USD/JPY closed 159.56 in New York on 27 Nov (DZH; +0.31; Fisco 159.60) after a 159.43 low and a 159.60 high at the London fix, with US 2-year 4.69% (+8 bp) and 10-year 4.94% (+9 bp) after the holiday; 159.36 in Tokyo at 17:09. No MoF or Katayama comment was found; the Fisco afternoon note cited caution over coordinated intervention near 160. The Fed: CME 97.4% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec. The Bank of Korea raised to 3.25% on 26 Nov (5–2) and lifted its 2027 growth forecast to 4.1%. The balance sheet was ¥626.2tn at 31 Oct. Next: Ueda in Nagoya (10:05) and the ¥2.8tn 2-year auction 30 Nov, October hard data 30 Nov and 1 Dec, 10-year auction 3 Dec, GDP second estimate 8 Dec, 30-year auction 8 Dec, FOMC 8–9 Dec, national CPI and the BoJ decision 18 Dec."},"cbName":"BoJ stance","conflicts":[{"a":"The leading data still say Expansion: the November flash manufacturing PMI rose to 53.7 with export orders at their second-fastest growth since January 2018, October exports were a record ¥11.77tn (+20.5%), core machinery orders +13.4% y/y, the September leading index was revised up to 116.5, the October services PPI printed +4.2% y/y, and the Bank of Korea raised its 2027 growth forecast to 4.1% on the same chip cycle.","b":"The household leg is turning: services PMI 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low, the composite at a six-month low, Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized on consumption −0.3% q/q, consumer confidence 35.0, Tokyo energy prices +9.5% in November as the subsidies ended, housing starts expected at −6.7% y/y for October, and the equity drawdown is 19.4% on the 27 Nov close.","read":"Leading over coincident keeps the regime Expansion on the manufacturing leg; the services side has not crossed 50 and the October hard data are not in yet, so the conflict is open.","trigger":"Services PMI under 50 in the final (3 Dec), consumer confidence under 35 on 2 Dec, or a second negative GDP quarter on 8 Dec moves the regime to Slowdown; October industrial production and retail sales on 30 Nov (expected +2.1% m/m and +2.7% y/y) and labour on 1 Dec are the first hard test."},{"a":"Breadth and volatility say the fall is over: 926 Prime names rose against 582 on 27 Nov after 1,164 against 345 in the morning, 26 of 33 sectors gained, the Nikkei VI closed 28.91 after two closes under 35, the index closed above its 5-day average for the first time since 13 Nov, and Wall Street's half-session added 1.6% to 2.3% with the SOX +4.69% and the VIX at 23.81.","b":"The index says it is not: the 1,212-point gain was again Advantest and Tokyo Electron (about 555 points) with the top five about 738, the 25-day and 13-week averages are still falling with the Ichimoku bearish pattern intact (Fisco), the close of 58,711.11 is 2.2% under the 60,030 first-test line and TOPIX 2.1% under 3,895, and the Huang testimony (1 and 3 Dec) and the Nvidia Southeast Asia and Shanghai reports keep the governance strand open.","read":"Two broad up-sessions and a VI under 30 are the best read since the fall began, and the lines have still not been retaken on a close; the framework waits for the close, not the breadth.","trigger":"A close above 60,030 with TOPIX above 3,895 and the Nikkei VI under 35 moves the posture to Neutral; a close under 56,436.65 is a new drawdown low."},{"a":"The Bank says the pace may need to speed up: Tamura on 26 Nov said 25 bp every few months is his base case and 50 bp \"may become necessary\", the Tokyo core CPI printed 2.9% on 27 Nov, a third month above 2%, Goldman now expects hikes in December, March and June to 2.0%, and the 2-year rose 3 bp to 1.760%.","b":"The market is still pricing part of a hold: MogWatch OIS stayed at 74.7% for December through the CPI release with March at 1.595% and 1.50% the modal March outcome (45.4%), Tamura himself said December \"or a later meeting\", the Tokyo core missed the 3.0% consensus with core-core easing to 2.5%, ANZ calls aggressive tightening unwarranted, and the 2-year's rise was squaring before the 30 Nov auction rather than repricing.","read":"The print changed no pricing in either direction; the market is waiting for the governor in Nagoya, and the 2-year between 1.70% and 1.85% is the range that says December is the base case without the 2027 pace.","trigger":"A 2-year close above 1.85% prices the 2027 pace; a market-stability reference from Ueda on 30 Nov is the hold tell; a 2-year close under 1.70% says the hold is the base case; a 2-year auction tail wider than 1 bp on 30 Nov is a demand warning."},{"a":"The yen is off the haven path and trading the US curve: USD/JPY closed 159.56 on 27 Nov (+0.31) with its 159.60 high at the month-end London fix as US 2-year and 10-year yields rose 8 and 9 bp, the front-end gap is about 293 bp with the Fed at 97.4% for 25 bp, and neither a 2.9% Tokyo core CPI nor a member talking about 50 bp produced a bid.","b":"The line is close and the MoF has the data: 160 is 44 sen away, the 13 Nov high was 160.18, Fisco's afternoon note cited caution over coordinated US-Japan intervention near 160, Gaitame marks 159.60 as the pivot, Nomura forecasts 154 in a month, the November intervention total prints 30 Nov at 19:00, and the broad equity rebound removes the risk-off excuse for yen weakness.","read":"The pair is carry and month-end flows in thin liquidity; the next move is the MoF's or Ueda's, not the data's, and 160 is the event.","trigger":"A close above 160 brings the MoF; a return of the broad equity fall (TOPIX under 3,674) or a US liquidation that holds takes the pair toward 158.4 and the 155 to 156 carry-unwind zone."},{"a":"The super-long end says the stress has eased: the 30-year closed 3.870% on 27 Nov (−2 bp) and the 40-year 4.090% (−2.5 bp), a third day lower, primary dealers told the MoF super-long issuance should stay flat with any FY2027 increase in shorter maturities (QUICK readout), the 30-year is under the 3.90% half of the pair-off condition, and the liquidity-supply auction drew 3.89 cover with a 0.1 bp tail.","b":"The evidence is supply expectations, not demand: the 26 Nov rally was the BoJ operation absorbing heavy selling (4.43 cover), the 25 Nov 40-year auction cleared above consensus on 2.63 cover, the MoF shelved the 2-year floater for want of demand, the investor-meeting readout is not public, MUFG expects FY2027 issuance up by the low ¥10tns, the food tax cut (about ¥5tn a year, unfunded) is in the Upper House to 12 Dec, and the yen is at 159.56, not under 158.5.","read":"Lower super-long yields on the promise of flat issuance lower the gauge without testing it; the warn pair stays on because the yen half is unmet, and the 30-year auction on 8 Dec is the first real demand test.","trigger":"A 30-year close above 4.05% while equities fall is the term-premium signal and 4.30% the fiscal line; a close under 3.90% with USD/JPY under 158.5 turns the warn pair off; the 30-year auction on 8 Dec and the year-end issuance plan are the demand tests."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-11-27","impact":"The chip rebound and the yield rise arrived together: Tokyo's AI leaders get the SOX tailwind on 30 Nov while the yen gets 8 to 9 bp more of US front-end carry, which put USD/JPY at 159.56, 44 sen from the MoF's line, on the month-end fix.","src":"CappNotes closing look; STL.News; StockTitan; Yahoo Finance AlphaCheck via AOL; DZH via Diamond Zai FX and Myforex; Fisco via Zaikei 879546 and Investing.com Japan","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street 27 Nov (half-session, 1 pm close): S&P 500 7,297.49 (+114.71, +1.60%), Nasdaq 24,129.50 (+543.70, +2.31%), Dow 50,774.94 (+591.99, +1.18%; up more than 640 intraday), Russell 2000 2,750.30 (+1.71%), VIX 23.81 (−15.8%), Philadelphia Semiconductor Index +4.69%, S&P advancers 406 to 94, technology +2.80%; Nvidia +0.80% to $150.49 after an early dip on the FT report that it halted shipments to Southeast Asia and a Bloomberg report of Blackwell chips installed at a Shanghai data centre, AMD +4.56% despite an Erste downgrade, Micron +7.60%, Broadcom +3.82%, Sandisk +15.96%, Dell +6.70%, Marvell +7.34%, Tesla +5.22%, Anthropic $91.88 (+10.98%, above the $83 IPO price); weekly S&P 500 +5.60% (best since April 2025), Dow +5.46% (largest since October 2022), Nasdaq +6.68%. Treasuries (2 pm close): 2-year 4.69% (+8 bp), 10-year 4.94% (+9 bp), 30-year 5.33% (+9 bp) after October wholesale inventories +0.9% (consensus +0.5%) and an advance goods deficit of $149.5bn (consensus $136.0bn); dollar index 104.04 (+0.36%); CME 97.4% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec, Polymarket 94.5%. USD/JPY closed 159.56 (DZH, +0.31; Fisco 159.60) after a 159.43 low and a 159.60 high at the London fix; EUR/USD 1.0936 (−0.0033) after soft French and Spanish flash CPI and an S&P negative outlook on France; EUR/JPY 174.36. WTI January $83.08 (−0.72%, fourth weekly loss), Brent January $95.44 (+0.88%) after a projectile hit Saudi Aramco's Jazan refinery; gold $3,668.50 (+3.75%), silver +9.11%, copper +5.03%; OPEC+ reported to hold targets through Q1 2027 ahead of its 29 Nov meeting; Adobe: $7.1bn Thanksgiving online spending (+10.4%).","url":"https://cappnotes.substack.com/p/closing-look-friday-november-27-2026"},{"date":"2026-11-27","impact":"A 2.11% gain on 60% breadth with the VI under 30 for the first time in the drawdown: still led by two names, still 2.2% under the 60,030 line, but the second broad up-session in a row and the first close above the 5-day line in two weeks.","src":"Kabutan n202611270951 and n202611270945; Fisco via Zaikei 879504, 879530 and 879492; media-ir 189674; NERO (yen at 17:09); investing.com snapshot (futures)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close 27 Nov: Nikkei 58,711.11 (+1,211.95, +2.11%), open 57,669.79 (+170.63), briefly 52 points under the prior close, high 58,738.02 near the close, low 57,447.16; the first close above the 5-day average since 13 Nov (Fisco), with the 25-day and 13-week lines still falling; TOPIX 3,815.20 (+64.80, +1.73%); Prime 926 up, 582 down (59.7% advancing), 26 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, glass and ceramics, oil and coal, electrical equipment, machinery) against services, land transport, retail, other products and real estate; turnover ¥7,787.2bn on 2,401.89m shares. Contributors (15:30): Advantest 26,585 (+1,755, +378.64 points), Tokyo Electron 8,220 (+349, +176.47), SoftBank Group 2,984.5 (+90.5, +73.22), Ibiden 5,120 (+452, +60.95), TDK 2,306 (+97, +49.05), Kioxia 8,450 (+688, +48.70; second TOPIX float-ratio increase), Fanuc 5,339 (+245, +41.30), Lasertec 33,600 (+3,050, +41.13), Toyota Tsusho +27.41, Fujikura 2,199.5 (+24.78), Murata 5,087 (+21.36), Shin-Etsu +20.90, Taiyo Yuden +17.83, SCREEN +16.59, HOYA 24,325 (+14.83), Disco 44,280 (+14.02); Recruit 16,700 (−545, −55.12), Konami 30,470 (−720, −24.27), Fast Retailing 85,330 (−170, −13.75), Terumo −12.00, Bandai Namco −9.71, Kikkoman −9.52, KDDI −7.28, Daikin −6.07, Sony 4,413 (−5.90); 160 constituents up, 64 down; the top five added about 738 points, the bottom five about 115. Nikkei VI 28.91 (−2.22, −7.13%) after 42.35 intraday; Growth 250 680.38 (+27.78); futures 58,817.50 (+930) on an untimed snapshot. Drivers (Kabutan, media-ir): DAX +2.08% and FTSE +1.75% on 26 Nov with the US closed, Nasdaq 100 futures and Korean stocks up, futures bought in the lunch break, calm domestic long rates after the Tokyo CPI miss, interim-dividend reinvestment, buying back of chip-equipment and component names; JT up on a reported bid, Mitsubishi Heavy on a report of ¥56.3tn defence spending; concerns noted on AI investment returns, a further BoJ hike and US tariffs. USD/JPY 159.36 at 17:09 Tokyo.","url":"https://s.kabutan.jp/news/n202611270951/"},{"date":"2026-11-27","impact":"The front end sold into the 2-year auction and the super-long end kept easing on the dealer readout that issuance there stays flat: the 2-year at 1.760% prices December as the base case again, and the 30-year under 3.90% is a supply story, not a demand one, with the yen still at 159.56.","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (news and table); Fisco via Zaikei 879482; MoF reference yields CSV; MoF auction calendars (November and December 2026)","tag":"Markets","title":"JGB closes 27 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, 15:28): 2-year 1.760% (+3 bp on QUICK's revised 1.730% for 26 Nov; 1.745% at 11:00; Fisco 1.745%), 5-year 2.065% (+1.5 bp), 10-year 2.640% (+1 bp; the SMTB table shows +0.5 bp; morning low 2.625% after the Tokyo CPI; Fisco 2.632%), 20-year 3.560% (+0.5 bp), 30-year 3.870% (−2 bp), 40-year 4.090% (−2.5 bp); December futures 128.12 (−12 sen), open 128.23, high 128.41, low 128.09; TONA December 98.5625 (unchanged). The ¥500bn liquidity-supply auction (5 to 11 years) drew 3.89 cover (3.77 last time) with a 0.1 bp gap between the highest and average yields, called somewhat strong by a domestic strategist with little secondary reaction. Drivers: the Tokyo core CPI miss (2.9% against QUICK's 3.0%) supported bonds in the morning; the Nikkei's 1,200-point gain, US 10-year yields near 4.92% in Tokyo hours and WTI in the mid-$85s weighed in the afternoon; mid-curve selling was partly squaring before the ¥2.8tn 2-year auction on 30 Nov; AI uncertainty remains a background bid. MoF primary-dealer meeting readout (26 Nov, QUICK): participants said super-long issuance should stay flat given the past cuts, some said certain tenors had room for further cuts, and any FY2027 increase would likely fall in other tenors, which gave the market comfort; no readout of the 27 Nov investor meeting was found. MoF reference yields for 26 Nov: 2-year 1.747%, 5-year 2.073%, 10-year 2.704%, 20-year 3.615%, 30-year 3.956%, 40-year 4.049% (the 27 Nov row not yet published). MoF December calendar: 10-year 3 Dec, 30-year 8 Dec, 5-year 10 Dec, liquidity supply 15 Dec (5 to 11 years) and 22 Dec (11 to 39 years), 20-year 17 Dec, 2-year 24 Dec.","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"date":"2026-11-27","impact":"A Tokyo core at 2.9% on energy with core-core easing moved December pricing by nothing: the market is waiting for Ueda on 30 Nov, and the 2-year's 3 bp rise is auction supply rather than repricing; the BoK hike is the regional confirmation that the chip cycle is inflationary.","src":"MogWatch (OIS model); NERO Signal; Pound Sterling Live; FinScans citing FXStreet; Reuters via AOL (Bank of Korea)","tag":"Policy","title":"BoJ pricing after the Tokyo CPI: MogWatch OIS 74.7% for a hike on 18 Dec (hold 25.3%) on trades to 21:06 JST on 27 Nov, unchanged from the 00:10 JST reading before the release (73.5% at 13:07 JST per NERO's reading of the model; 76.7% on 26 Nov, 84.7% on 20 Nov); market-implied rate 1.437% for December (1.459% a week earlier), 1.484% January, 1.595% March (1.632%), 1.641% April, 1.778% June; average expected change +18.7 bp; March distribution 1.25% 11.4%, 1.50% 45.4%, 1.75% 36.9%, 2.00% 6.2%. Pound Sterling Live: money markets priced about 17 bp for December on 25 Nov, roughly two in three; Nomura: the December possibility \"remains high\", USD/JPY 154 in one month and 150 in three; Goldman Sachs: three 25 bp hikes in December 2026, March 2027 and June 2027 to a 2.0% terminal rate; ANZ (Martin): the backdrop \"does not warrant aggressive tightening\". FinScans/FXStreet: 19 bp priced for 18 Dec (27 Nov, 14:44 GMT). Bank of Korea (26 Nov): 7-day repo rate raised 25 bp to 3.25%, the highest since November 2024, by 5–2, the third hike this year; 2026 growth forecast 3.9% from 3.3%, 2027 4.1% from 2.9%, 2027 inflation 2.4%; analysts see a final hike to 3.50% in Q1 2027.","url":"https://mogwatch.jp/v2/policy/jpy"},{"date":"2026-11-27","impact":"The early read for the 18 Dec national CPI is above target on energy with the underlying pace cooling, which keeps December priced near three-quarters and gives the hawks the headline and the market the core-core; no market moved on it.","src":"MIC via OANDA Japan, investingLive, ActionForex and Trading Economics","tag":"Data","title":"Tokyo November CPI (MIC, 08:30): core (ex fresh food) +2.9% y/y from +2.6%, the highest since July 2025 and a third month above 2%, against a 3.0% consensus (Reuters, QUICK; OANDA 2.9%); headline +2.8% from +2.7%; ex fresh food and energy +2.5% from +2.6%; energy +9.5% from +2.2% (electricity +9.5%, +14.2% m/m; city gas +13.7%, +13.0% m/m; gasoline −1.1%) as the subsidies that still covered September usage in October ended, adding about 0.34 pt to the core change; fresh food +1.8% from +8.6%; food ex fresh +3.1% (+3.0%), eating out +4.7%, prepared food +4.8%, meat +6.4%, non-Koshihikari rice −33.8%; services +2.1% (+2.2%), accommodation +3.6% (+6.7%), rent +1.9%; childcare fees −0.21 pt to March 2027; seasonally adjusted m/m: headline +0.3%, core +0.5%, core-core +0.1%. Reaction: USD/JPY 159.27 at 08:37 after 159.26 to 159.28 in the first minute; Nikkei 57,758 at 08:37 from 57,798; JGB futures opened −1 sen and traded to 128.35.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/tokyocpi-nov-2026-review/"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"The most hawkish member put 50 bp on the table and December \"or later\" in the same afternoon; the 2-year rose 1.5 bp to 1.740%, the yen dipped to 158.95 and recovered, and OIS slipped to 74.7% for December, so the market took the pace talk as a view and the \"or later\" as the signal.","src":"Reuters via MarketScreener; Jiji via nippon.com (EN and JP); Kyodo via OANDA Japan; ANN via ABEMA","tag":"Policy","title":"Tamura (Kofu, 26 Nov). Speech (10:00): the BoJ \"needs to bring its policy rate to a level deemed neutral to the economy soon\" because financial conditions remain accommodative; with underlying inflation less firmly anchored at 2% than in the US or Europe the Bank \"must respond swiftly to signs of an upward deviation in prices, including by accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\"; \"not to go slowly, but unhurried haste\" (悠々として急ぐ); upside risks from Middle East raw-material costs, import prices on the weak yen and AI demand. Press conference (14:00, Reuters via MarketScreener, Jiji via nippon.com): a hike will be discussed \"at the December or a later meeting\" and there is a good chance of one within about three months of the September move; \"there's still about three weeks until our December policy meeting, so I would like to scrutinise various data\"; 25 bp \"once every few months\" is the base case and \"if upside price risks still exist, a 50-basis-point rate hike may become necessary\"; neutral about 2%; the AI-led stock correction could affect corporate and household decisions and the real economy; the effects of each hike can be assessed within a few months, which \"does not mean slowly\".","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/boj-s-tamura-signals-chance-of-faster-pace-interest-rate-hikes-ce7851d8dd81ff25"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"A 1,240-point AI rebound that closed as 488 on flat breadth, with the VI collapsing from 43 to 31 by the close: a two-way market in four names with the broad market unchanged, 4.2% under the first-test line.","src":"Kabutan n202611260984; Fisco via Zaikei 879302 and 879295; Gentosha Gold Online; OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045983; Fisco via Investing.com 1771495 and 1771569; Nikkei headline (night futures)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close 26 Nov: Nikkei 57,499.16 (+487.54, +0.86%), open 57,429.54, high 58,251.93 (up more than 1,240 before noon), low 57,288.10 in the afternoon; TOPIX 3,750.40 (+4.20, +0.11%); Prime 749 up, 751 down, 17 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, oil and coal, information and communication, glass, electrical equipment) against air transport, food, pharmaceuticals, insurance and construction; turnover ¥6,828.7bn on 2,242.27m shares. Contributors (15:30): Advantest 24,830 (+1,085, +234.09 points), SoftBank Group 2,894 (+197.5, +7.3%, +159.79), Kioxia 7,762 (+867, +12.57%, +61.38), TDK 2,209 (+33.88), Tokyo Electron 7,871 (+46, +23.26), Recruit +22.75, Ibiden 4,668 (+21.04), Nitto Denko +17.36, Lasertec 30,550 (+16.52), Murata +15.37, Disco +10.85; Fast Retailing 85,500 (−670, −54.21), Daikin 23,860 (−17.87), Chugai −12.14, Toyota Tsusho −11.93, Otsuka −11.12, Sony 4,448 (−10.96), HOYA −6.83; Nikkei constituents 110 up, 113 down. Nikkei VI 31.13 (−2.26, −6.77%) after 43.22 intraday (42.03 at 14:15); December futures 57,350 (+310), night session 57,890 (+540, Nikkei headline); Growth 250 652.60 (+12.00); Meiko +13.95%, MediaDo +13.82% on a dividend increase, Taisei −3.87%. Fade: a report that Nvidia asked manufacturing partners to halt server shipments to four Southeast Asian countries, the December Huang testimony, no appetite to chase before Thanksgiving, defensives sold, Tamura.","url":"https://s.kabutan.jp/news/n202611260984/"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"The front end sold on Tamura while the super-long end rallied into the Bank's bid: a twist that leaves the 2-year under 1.75% and the fiscal gauge unchanged, since the 30-year fell on central-bank absorption rather than demand.","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB; Fisco via Zaikei 879296; QUICK Money World headline; Reuters via MarketScreener and FixedIncomeNews (floating-rate bond)","tag":"Markets","title":"JGB closes 26 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, 15:27): 2-year 1.740% (+1.5 bp; Fisco 1.726%), 5-year 2.055% (+2.5 bp), 10-year 2.645% (+2.5 bp against a revised 2.620% for 25 Nov, high 2.660%; SMTB table 2.635%, Fisco 2.615%), 20-year 3.560% (−2 bp), 30-year 3.895% (−5.5 bp), 40-year 4.115% (−6.5 bp); December futures 128.24 (unchanged), range 127.96 to 128.41; TONA December futures 98.5625 (−0.0100). The BoJ purchase operation drew 4.43 cover in the 10- to 25-year bucket (3.58 last time, the highest since June), 2.51 in 3- to 5-year and 4.59 in linkers, read by a domestic strategist as weak on heavy investor selling. The MoF primary-dealer meeting on the FY2027 plan heard requests for increases and decreases by maturity (QUICK Money World headline; no readout in the pages opened); the debut 2-year floating-rate bond planned for January is postponed pending demand (Reuters, 17 Nov), with the plan due at year-end and a June review. 27 Nov open: futures 128.23 (−1 sen), high 128.35 after the Tokyo CPI; TONA 98.5700 (+0.0075); European yields rose on 26 Nov; the ¥500bn liquidity-supply auction (5 to 11 years) and the investor meeting follow.","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"A formal protest with no retaliation and a restatement that 15% is the deal: the export leg is unchanged until a rate takes effect in 2027, and the leading-index revision is a touch firmer.","src":"Novaist (Kihara); Sustainable Japan (USTR terms); Myforex DZH dzh261126130 (index revisions); OANDA Japan mn_1045961 (yen)","tag":"Trade","title":"Section 301 reaction and data, 26 Nov: Chief Cabinet Secretary Kihara (morning press conference) called Japan's inclusion in the USTR proposal regrettable (遺憾) and said this had been conveyed to the US side, that Japan has no structural overcapacity and no non-market policies or practices, that Japan and the US have cooperated on third-country overcapacity, that the US has agreed not to impose tariffs beyond the 2025 agreement, which the proposal reflects (15% in total net of MFN and the existing 12.5% duty, Section 232 goods exempt, 20% alternative if commitments are judged insufficient; comments to 5 Jan, hearing 25 Jan 2027), and that Japan will steadily implement the deal including the US investment. Cabinet Office (14:00): the September leading index was revised to 116.5 from the 116.4 preliminary and the coincident index held at 119.5. Bank of Korea decided on rates the same day (expected 3.25% from 3.00%, Fisco preview; result not in the pages opened). USD/JPY 159.16 at 15:00 Tokyo (158.95 to 159.26).","url":"https://novaist.jp/articles/japan-protests-us-tariff-plan/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"For Japan the headline rate matches the July 2025 deal and autos are exempt, so the export leg is unchanged until a rate takes effect in 2027; the 20% alternative tied to the $550bn investment commitments is the new political risk. It pushed US futures down overnight before PCE.","src":"Wiley Rein alert; Baker McKenzie Global Import Blog; Reuters via Korea Times; BigGo Finance; coki.jp","tag":"Trade","title":"USTR (25 Nov, US time) issued affirmative Section 301 determinations on structural excess capacity and proposed tariffs on 14 economies: Japan 15% in total net of MFN and the existing 12.5% July 2026 tariff, with an alternative of up to 20% if consultations on the deal commitments are judged unsatisfactory; the EU 15% (alternative 20%), Korea and Taiwan 15% (25%), Switzerland 15% (20%), China up to 20% with Annex A goods 10% and Annex B exempt (up to 40% if engagement fails), Vietnam up to 35% (20% with an agreement), India up to 25% (17%), Singapore up to 20% (10%), Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand and Cambodia up to 18% (25%), Bangladesh up to 18% (20%); Mexico and Norway still under investigation. For the 13 non-China economies goods under Section 232 (autos and parts, steel, aluminium, copper, semiconductors, patented pharmaceuticals, wood) are exempt and the new rate is reduced by the forced-labour 301 duties, so the duties do not stack. Comments and hearing requests due 5 Jan 2027; hearing from 25 Jan 2027 at the ITC; final action expected early 2027. Greer: the caps match the deals already reached. The report cites Japan's industrial plans, BoJ-era financial repression, NISA, zombie firms, the technical-intern system and Rapidus and JASM subsidies (coki). No Japanese government reaction was found.","url":"https://www.wiley.law/alert-USTR-Announces-Affirmative-Section-301-Determinations-and-Proposes-New-Tariffs-on-14-Trading-Partners-to-Address-Structural-Excess-Capacity"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"A late recovery after a morning liquidation in AI names, hot core PCE and the Section 301 proposal: yields fell anyway, which is the haven trade, and the Fed hike is fully priced. Tokyo took the recovery, not the fall, on 26 Nov.","src":"CappNotes closing look; Motley Fool; GSM Global; OANDA Japan NY digest mn_1045891; Fisco via Zaikei 879162 (US data)","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street 25 Nov: S&P 500 7,182.78 (−18.31, −0.25%) after −3.2% intraday, Nasdaq 23,585.80 (−147.74, −0.62%) after −4.6%, Dow 50,182.95 (+164.25, +0.33%) after a fall of more than 1,100, Russell 2000 2,704.06 (+0.71%), VIX 28.29 (+7.3%) after topping 30; Nvidia $149.30 (−$6.08, −3.91%, low $141.17), AMD $406.03 (−1.75%), Micron −4.53%, Broadcom $300.86 (−0.07%), Anthropic $82.79 (+1.07%, under the $83 IPO), HP +11.49%; SOXX −0.50%, SMH −1.01% (SOX close not found). Data: October PCE +0.3% m/m and 3.8% y/y, core PCE +0.3% m/m and 3.3% y/y (consensus 3.2%, September 3.1%); Q3 GDP second estimate 4.3% annualized (consensus 3.5%, advance 3.4%) with consumption 4.2%; durable goods +0.2% (consensus +0.7%), ex-transport +0.5%; initial claims 189,000 (200,000); new home sales 701,000 (650,000); personal income +0.7%. US 2-year 4.61% (−2 bp), 10-year 4.85% (−6 to −8 bp; 4.79% low), 30-year 5.24%; CME FedWatch 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec (Gaitame 96.4%; Polymarket 94%, Kalshi 93%); dollar index 103.71 (−0.10%); WTI $83.68 (+1.99%), Brent $94.61 (+3.51%) after a Houthi strike on Ras Tanura; gold $3,534.70 (−0.71%), under $3,500 intraday for the first time since August 2025. US markets closed 26 Nov, early close 27 Nov.","url":"https://www.fool.com/coverage/stock-market-today/2026/11/25/stock-market-today-nov-25-nvidia-slides-3-9-as-the-market-considers-hot-pce-inflation-data-and-new-tariff-proposals/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Bull-flattening to 20 years on the haven bid and a hold being priced at the front end, while the super-long end cheapened on supply: the two halves of the curve are now sending opposite signals and the 40-year at 4.18% is the fiscal gauge into the MoF meetings.","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB; Fisco via Zaikei 879100; stock-marketdata.com; Newsquawk","tag":"Markets","title":"JGB closes 25 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, 15:36): 2-year 1.720% (−5.5 bp; Fisco 1.722%), 5-year 2.030% (−8 bp), 10-year 2.645% (−7.5 bp, lowest in about three weeks; Fisco 2.627%, stock-marketdata.com 2.620%), 20-year 3.580% (−4.5 bp; Fisco 3.556%), 30-year 3.950% (+1.5 bp), new 40-year 4.180% (+6 bp); December futures 128.24 (+64 sen), open 127.66, high 128.39 (highest since 6 Nov). The ¥300bn 40-year reopening (19th issue, 3.8% coupon, results 12:35) cleared at a 4.09% highest accepted yield, above the 4.070% to 4.080% consensus, on 2.63 cover against 3.10 in September, called somewhat weak by a domestic strategist on investor demand; the new 40-year traded 4.145% before the sale. Drivers: the Nikkei down more than 2,000 intraday, AI selling on Nasdaq on 24 Nov, US 10-year under 4.90% in Tokyo hours. MoF primary-dealer meeting 26 Nov, investor meeting 27 Nov.","url":"https://www.zaikei.co.jp/article/20261125/879100.html"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The index fell 2.78% while 64% of the market rose: the drawdown is confined to the AI complex and the close held 575 points above the 20 Nov liquidation low. The posture stays Risk-off on the index, with breadth the best since the fall began.","src":"TBL Advisory 20261125nk; Tokyo Hodo Shimbun 30906; OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045754; QUICK Money World 223679; Fisco via Zaikei 879109 (VI); Kabutan headline (night session)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close 25 Nov: Nikkei 57,011.62 (−1,633.01, −2.78%), open 58,383.49, low 56,502.97 (down more than 2,100 in the morning; morning close 56,710.43), Prime turnover ¥7.4977tn on 2,734m shares, Prime 993 up, 537 down, 22 flat (63.9% advancing), 23 of 33 sectors up: pharmaceuticals +5.48%, real estate +3.54%, food +3.53%, retail +2.95% against nonferrous metals −8.69%, electrical equipment −4.48%, glass and ceramics −3.79%. Advantest about −762 index points and Tokyo Electron −567 (together about 1,328), SoftBank Group −379 points (−14.8%, year-to-date low), Ibiden −115, Fujikura 2,062.5 (−14.7%, downgraded), Murata 4,633 (−14.2%); Fast Retailing 86,170 (+2,130, +172 points), Chugai 7,501 (+426, +43), Terumo up, Ono Pharmaceutical 2,699 (+10.7%) on an Amgen patent settlement. Nikkei VI 33.39 (+1.88, +5.97%; high 43.23). December futures 57,040 (−1,940); night session opened 56,660 and closed 57,370 (Kabutan headline). TOPIX 3,746.20 (−18.68, −0.50%), Growth 250 640.60 (−14.24). USD/JPY 159.21 to 159.23 at 17:00.","url":"https://tbladvisory.com/20261125nk/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Services prices are running well above 2% at the corporate level, which supports the BoJ's December case even as the market trims it to about 78%.","src":"Bank of Japan","tag":"Data","title":"October services PPI (BoJ, 08:50): +4.2% y/y from a revised 3.8%, the fastest since March 1990, m/m +1.1%; ex-international transport +3.2% (3.0%); international transport +99.1% (ocean freight +120.4%), transport and postal +9.4%, accommodation +12.1%, leasing and rental +11.8%, real estate +3.0%, information and communications +2.5%, advertising −1.2%.","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/statistics/pi/cspi_release/sppi2610.pdf"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Manufacturing keeps the regime in Expansion and the services slide is the first survey evidence of the equity fall reaching households; the composite at a six-month low with confidence at a 2020 low is the warn for the household leg.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com; ActionForex; au.investing; IndexBox","tag":"Data","title":"November flash PMIs (S&P Global / au Jibun Bank, 08:30): manufacturing 53.7 from 53.3 (consensus 53.5), output 53.8 from 53.0, new orders solid, manufacturing export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018 on Asian semiconductor and electronics demand, employment up, outstanding work rising a twelfth month; services 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5 from 52.1, a six-month low; business confidence joint-lowest since August 2020; input costs an eight-month low, output charges the weakest since March. Fiddes: 'momentum was lost amid a slowdown in service sector activity'.","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661847-japan-pmi-growth-slows-to-six-month-low-as-services-weaken-despite-manufacturing-strength/"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The governance shock that drove the 20 Nov liquidation reversed in one session; the SEC and DOJ inquiries continue (Al Jazeera), so the AI chain's governance strand is open even as the price has recovered.","src":"Nvidia newsroom and SEC 8-K; Al Jazeera; Motley Fool; Fox Business; STL.News; TBL Advisory","tag":"Markets","title":"Nvidia's board 'voted unanimously' to reinstate Jensen Huang as president, CEO and director effective immediately, citing 'input from senior levels of the United States government', 'no national security risk' and satisfaction with his 'candor', with an independent-director investigation of the reported foreign-intelligence effort, a new National Security and Export Control Committee, a chief compliance officer search and continued cooperation with government investigations; Kress continues as CFO. Trump: 'the correct and proper decision', he was 'represented in the board meeting'. Nvidia $165.05 (+$39.52, +31.48%, range $156.93 to $171.50, 772.5m shares, about $3.03tn; largest one-day percentage gain in more than two decades per Motley Fool); S&P 500 7,237.17 (+326.78, +4.73%), Nasdaq 24,089.56 (+1,471.82, +6.51%), Dow 49,595.31 (+1,447.98, +3.01%), Russell 2000 2,669.66 (+3.40%), VIX 26.62 from 35.89; Micron +19%, AMD $453.46 (+6.85%), Broadcom $313.55 (+3.00%), Oracle +12.34%, Tesla +11.5%, Meta +6.6%, Anthropic $87.64 (−1.4%). Chicago Nikkei futures 57,960.","url":"https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-board-reinstates-jensen-huang-as-president-ceo-and-board-director"}],"headline":"Tokyo closed the week at 58,711.11 (+1,211.95, +2.11%) on Advantest and Tokyo Electron with 60% of Prime up and the Nikkei VI at 28.91, leaving the Nikkei 2.2% under the 60,030 first-test line and TOPIX (3,815.20, +1.73%) 2.1% under 3,895; Wall Street's half-session added 1.6% to 2.3% with the SOX +4.69%. JGBs sold at the front end into the 2-year auction (2-year 1.760%, +3 bp; 10-year 2.640%) while the super-long end eased (30-year 3.870%, 40-year 4.090%) and the liquidity-supply auction drew 3.89 cover; MogWatch OIS kept December at 74.7% after the Tokyo core CPI miss (2.9% against 3.0%). USD/JPY closed 159.56 in New York (+0.31) after a 159.60 high at the month-end fix, with US yields up 8 to 9 bp; Ueda speaks in Nagoya on 30 Nov with October industrial production, retail sales and the intervention total.","marketClose":"Fri 27 Nov 2026","notes":["This refresh ran at about 04:50 UTC (13:50 JST) on Saturday 28 Nov after the Friday close, so the Nikkei, TOPIX, JGB and USD/JPY rows and the history hold the 27 Nov closes (marketClose). The MoF investor-meeting readout (27 Nov), the 27 Nov MoF reference yields, an OANDA Japan New York digest for 27 Nov and a reported FY2026 supplementary budget (Mainichi and ANN headlines, pages blocked) were not available in any page opened.","Nikkei 27 Nov close 58,711.11 (+1,211.95, +2.11%), high 58,738.02, low 57,447.16, breadth 926 / 582, 26 sectors up, volume 2,401.89m shares and turnover ¥7,787.2bn are Kabutan (s.kabutan n202611270951 and n202611270945) and media-ir, which agree; the open 57,669.79 and the contributor table are Fisco via Zaikei 879504 (15:30 snapshot); TOPIX 3,815.20 (+64.80) is Kabutan and media-ir; the technical remarks are Fisco via Zaikei 879530; Nikkei VI 28.91 (−2.22; high 42.35) is Fisco via Zaikei 879492 at 16:35; Nikkei futures 58,817.50 (+930) is an untimed investing.com ticker snapshot and is not used in the rows; the OANDA Japan summary mn_1046188 returned a server error.","JGBs 27 Nov are QUICK/NQN 15:28 levels via SMTB (2-year 1.760%, 5-year 2.065%, 10-year 2.640%, 20-year 3.560%, 30-year 3.870%, 40-year 4.090%), described as still trading at 15:00; the futures close 128.12 (−12 sen) and TONA 98.5625 are from the same feed. QUICK's 27 Nov changes imply 26 Nov closes of 1.730% (2-year), 2.630% (10-year) and 3.890% (30-year) against the 1.740%, 2.645% and 3.895% its 26 Nov item reported and the previous refresh recorded; the rows carry QUICK's revised basis as the prior and the changes are QUICK's. The SMTB table shows the 10-year at 2.640% (+0.5 bp). Fisco via Zaikei 879482 lists 2-year 1.745%, 5-year 2.051%, 10-year 2.632%, 20-year 3.533% without labelling them as closes. MoF reference yields (jgbcm.csv) for 26 Nov are 2-year 1.747%, 10-year 2.704%, 30-year 3.956%, 40-year 4.049%; the 27 Nov row was not yet in the file. The liquidity-supply auction size (¥500bn) is from the calendar, not the result item.","USD/JPY 159.56 for 27 Nov is DZH's New York close (via Diamond Zai FX and Myforex, +0.31); Fisco via Zaikei 879546 says the pair rose to 159.60 and closed there, so the sources differ by 4 sen; the 159.43 low is Fisco and the 159.60 high is both; the Tokyo 17:09 level (159.36) is NERO Signal. US index closes are CappNotes and STL.News, which agree; Treasury yields, VIX, sector moves, SOX, oil, gold and CME pricing are CappNotes; Nvidia's $150.49 close is StockTitan with CappNotes' +0.80%. The dollar index 104.04 is CappNotes (investing.com's untimed ticker shows 103.99).","BoJ pricing: MogWatch's 74.7% is on trades to 21:06 JST on 27 Nov (page data to 21:40 JST); NERO Signal's 73.5% is its own 13:07 JST reading of the same model and is recorded for the intraday move only. Pound Sterling Live's 17 bp is for 25 Nov and FinScans' 19 bp (citing FXStreet) for 27 Nov have no stated instrument or time. The MoF primary-dealer readout is QUICK/NQN via SMTB; the 27 Nov investor-meeting outline page (mof.go.jp 261127) returned a client error. October consensus figures are ING via TMGM and etnet via Longbridge (industrial production +2.1% m/m, +2.7% y/y; retail +2.7% y/y; housing starts −6.7% y/y; capex +4.3%), with MetaTrader's calendar showing +0.2% m/m for industrial production; no Japanese consensus for unemployment or the jobs ratio was found. Release dates follow the DZH domestic schedule (unemployment and jobs ratio 1 Dec) and the MoF auction calendars (10-year 3 Dec, 30-year 8 Dec)."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"Risk-off, with the first test now 2.2% away. Tokyo closed 27 Nov at 58,711.11 (+1,211.95, +2.11%), the largest gain since 24 Nov, after opening 57,669.79, dipping 52 points under the prior close and closing near the 58,738.02 high; the low was 57,447.16. TOPIX 3,815.20 (+64.80, +1.73%). Prime breadth was 926 up against 582 down (59.7% advancing) with 26 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, glass, oil and coal, electrical equipment, machinery) against services, land transport, retail, other products and real estate; turnover ¥7.79tn on 2,402m shares. The leaders were again the AI complex: Advantest 26,585 (+1,755, +379 index points), Tokyo Electron 8,220 (+349, +176), SoftBank Group 2,984.5 (+73), Ibiden 5,120 (+61), TDK +49, Kioxia 8,450 (+688, +49, on a TOPIX float-ratio increase), Fanuc +41, Lasertec 33,600 (+41); Recruit 16,700 (−545, −55 points), Konami −24, Fast Retailing 85,330 (−14); 160 Nikkei constituents rose against 64. Nikkei VI closed 28.91 (−2.22) after a 42.35 intraday spike; Growth 250 680.38 (+27.78). Drivers: Europe +1.6% to +2.1% on 26 Nov, Nasdaq 100 futures up during the lunch break, a calm JGB market after the Tokyo CPI miss, interim-dividend reinvestment, and buying back of chip and component names. Wall Street then rose in the half-session (S&P 500 +1.60%, Nasdaq +2.31%, SOX +4.69%, VIX 23.81), so the 30 Nov open has a tailwind. Under the framework the drawdown is 19.4% from the 72,831.73 record, the close is 2,274 points above the 20 Nov liquidation close (56,436.65), breadth has been broad on two sessions, and the Nikkei VI is under 35 for a second close. The posture stays Risk-off until a close retakes 60,030 and 3,895 with the VI under 35; the 27 Nov close is 2.2% and 2.1% short of those lines, with Ueda in Nagoya and the October hard data on 30 Nov and the Huang testimony on 1 and 3 Dec the next inputs."},"regime":{"label":"Expansion","note":"No new leading or coincident print since the 26 Nov index revisions (September leading index 116.5, coincident 119.5). The November flash PMIs stand: manufacturing 53.7 from 53.3, output 53.8, export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018; services 50.5 from 51.7, composite 51.5, a six-month low. The leading set is in expansion on the manufacturing leg: BoJ Tankan +24, Reuters Tankan +27 and +35, September core machinery orders +13.4% y/y with a +4.3% q/q Q4 plan, consumer confidence 35.0, housing starts −2.7%. Coincident: October exports +20.5% y/y to a record ¥11.77tn, Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized on consumption −0.3% q/q, industrial production +1.7%, real wages +2.2%, unemployment 2.4%, jobs ratio 1.19, retail +2.3%. Prices: October national core CPI 2.2%, at target; November Tokyo core 2.9% on energy +9.5% with core-core easing to 2.5%, so the household squeeze is energy, not demand. The first October hard data print 30 Nov: industrial production (ING and Longbridge expect +2.1% m/m and +2.7% y/y; MetaTrader shows +0.2% m/m), retail sales (+2.7% y/y expected) and housing starts (−6.7% y/y expected); unemployment and the jobs ratio follow on 1 Dec. The framework reads leading over coincident; manufacturing is at the top of its five-year range and the regime stays Expansion with the household side the weak leg and the services PMI 0.5 point from the warn band. A services PMI under 50 in the final (3 Dec) or the December flash, consumer confidence under 35 on 2 Dec, or a second negative GDP quarter on 8 Dec moves it to Slowdown."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"+0.4","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":50},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":55.1},{"l":"May 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":54.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":54.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":54.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":53.3},{"l":"Nov 26","v":53.7}],"latest":"53.7","name":"au Jibun Bank Manufacturing PMI","next":"1 Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 (flash)","prior":"53.3","read":"The November flash rose to 53.7 from 53.3 in October (consensus 53.5), the first rise since the 54.9 of August. Output 53.8 from 53.0, new orders grew at a solid pace and export orders had their second-fastest growth since January 2018 on semiconductor and electronics demand from other Asian economies; employment rose, led by manufacturing, and outstanding work grew for a twelfth month. Input cost inflation slowed to an eight-month low and output charges to the weakest since March. Business confidence across both sectors fell to its joint-lowest since August 2020, so the order books are strong and the mood is not. Well above 50 and at the top of the five-year range: good. The final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion, chip-led","src":"S&P Global flash via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex, au.investing and IndexBox (24 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Up; beat 53.5","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661847-japan-pmi-growth-slows-to-six-month-low-as-services-weaken-despite-manufacturing-strength/"},{"change":"−1.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":51.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":53.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":51},{"l":"May 26","v":50},{"l":"Jun 26","v":52.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":51.3},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":50.5}],"latest":"50.5","name":"au Jibun Bank Services PMI","next":"3 Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 (flash)","prior":"51.7","read":"The November flash fell to 50.5 from 51.7, the lowest since the 50.0 of May, with new business growth at a four-month low; the composite fell to 51.5 from 52.1, a six-month low, and activity still expanded for a twentieth month. Business confidence is at its joint-lowest since August 2020, with manufacturers more optimistic than services firms; S&P Global's Fiddes said 'momentum was lost amid a slowdown in service sector activity' and that 'uncertainty remains elevated'. This is the first survey taken through the equity fall and the first sign of it in the household-facing data. Still above 50 so the signal stays good by the 50 line, but it is 0.5 point from the warn band and the divergence with manufacturing (53.7) is the widest since the spring; the final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Just above 50; composite six-month low","src":"S&P Global flash via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex and au.investing (24 Nov)","trend":"down","trendWord":"New business four-month low","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/japans-factory-activity-expands-in-november-pmi-shows-5046775"},{"change":"+2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 23","v":12},{"l":"Mar 24","v":11},{"l":"Jun 24","v":13},{"l":"Sep 24","v":13},{"l":"Dec 24","v":14},{"l":"Mar 25","v":12},{"l":"Jun 25","v":13},{"l":"Sep 25","v":14},{"l":"Dec 25","v":15},{"l":"Mar 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":22},{"l":"Sep 26","v":24}],"latest":"+24","name":"Tankan Large Manufacturers DI","next":"15 Dec (Dec survey)","period":"Sep 2026 survey","prior":"+22","read":"Released 1 Oct. Large manufacturers improved for a sixth quarter to +24, the best since March 2018, while large non-manufacturers slipped to +35 from +37. The December outlook DI is +21 for manufacturers and +30 for non-manufacturers, so firms expect some cooling. FY2026 capex plans for large firms are +11.3%. The monthly Reuters Tankan (11 Nov, survey 27 Oct to 6 Nov) points the other way for December: manufacturers +27 from +22 (consensus +23), the highest since August 2021, non-manufacturers +35 from +23, the highest since June 2018, with electronics +39 on memory-chip demand and the February outlook +28 and +29; respondents cited rising prices and interest rates as the drag. The December BoJ Tankan is due mid-December.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = favorable equals unfavorable","signal":"good","signalWord":"Highest since 2018","src":"Bank of Japan; Reuters Tankan via investingLive and RTTNews","trend":"up","trendWord":"Improving","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/statistics/tk/yoshi/tk2609.htm"},{"change":"−0.4","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":37.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":37.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":37.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":40},{"l":"Mar 26","v":33.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":32.2},{"l":"May 26","v":33.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":33.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":34.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":35.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":35.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":35}],"latest":"35.0","name":"Consumer Confidence Index","next":"2 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"35.4","read":"October fell to 35.0 from 35.4, a touch under the 35.1 consensus and the lowest since July, with every component lower: livelihood 33.2 (33.8), willingness to buy durables 26.8 (27.3), income growth 40.4 (40.5), employment 39.4 (40.0). The index has been in the 32 to 36 band since the March drop from 40.0 when the Hormuz closure lifted fuel prices. The October Eco Watchers survey (10 Nov) was also soft: the current index fell to 46.1 from 47.0 (consensus 46.8), households 45.5 (−0.9), corporate 47.6 (+0.5), and the outlook index fell to 46.5 from 47.4. Energy prices turned positive again in the Tokyo CPI in October, which explains the slip; the November reading prints on 2 Dec.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = neutral line","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 40 band","src":"Cabinet Office via Trading Economics; Eco Watchers via RTTNews, FX.co and Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lowest since July","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"−5.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":12.5},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-6.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":16.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":13.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":24.7},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5.9},{"l":"Apr 26","v":15.6},{"l":"May 26","v":-1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":16.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":11.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":18.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":13.4}],"latest":"+13.4%","name":"Core Machinery Orders, y/y","next":"15 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+18.6%","read":"Released 19 Nov. Core orders (private, ex ships and electric power) fell 0.3% on the month to ¥1,066.2bn after +5.2% in August (Newsquawk consensus −0.5%, RTTNews +1.3%), the first fall in two months, and rose 13.4% from a year earlier against +13.7% expected and +18.6% in August. Manufacturers' orders rose 3.9% m/m to ¥547.6bn and non-manufacturers' ex utilities 1.1% to ¥547.2bn; government orders fell 1.3%, overseas orders rose 6.2%, total orders were flat. The Cabinet Office kept its 'signs of a pickup' assessment and the Q4 (Oct to Dec) forecast is +4.3% q/q for core orders, +2.6% for manufacturers, +5.2% for non-manufacturers and +8.8% for total orders. The series is noisy; the Tankan capex plans (+11.3% for large firms) and October machine tool orders (+42.5% y/y, the sixteenth straight gain) are the cleaner reads and point the same way. A double-digit y/y gain with a positive Q4 plan keeps the capex lead in place; the heat has come off the August beat.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Small miss; Q4 plan +4.3% q/q","src":"Cabinet Office via FX.co, RTTNews, Newsquawk, Kyodo (OANDA Japan) and Japan Planners (Q4 forecast)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower, still double-digit","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3299479"},{"change":"−8.8 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":3.2},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-8.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-1.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-0.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-4.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-29.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":11.4},{"l":"May 26","v":33.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":18.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":8.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-2.7}],"latest":"−2.7%","name":"Housing Starts, y/y","next":"30 Nov (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+6.1%","read":"Housing starts fell 2.7% y/y in September against a +2.9% consensus, the first fall since March after five positive months (+33.9% in May to +6.1% in August). Built-for-sale −4.5%, owned −3.4%, rented −1.6%, issued +18.4%. The comparison month, September 2025, was itself −7.3%, so the level is weak rather than flattered by the base. With the 10-year JGB near 2.8% and mortgage rates rising after three BoJ hikes, the housing leg of the leading set has turned down.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Falls on a weak base, missed","src":"MLIT via RTTNews and Newsquawk","trend":"down","trendWord":"Turns negative","url":"https://www.rttnews.com/3706092/japan-housing-starts-fall-2-7-in-september.aspx"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"−1.0 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2},{"l":"May 26","v":-2.1},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.7}],"latest":"+1.7%","name":"Industrial Production, y/y","next":"30 Nov (Oct prelim)","period":"Sep 2026 (final)","prior":"+2.7%","read":"September final +1.7% y/y (preliminary +1.9%) with the m/m rebound held at +2.0%. October prints 30 Nov at 08:50: ING and Longbridge expect +2.1% m/m and +2.7% y/y, MetaTrader's calendar shows +0.2% m/m, and the METI survey had +5.1% m/m. A print near the survey would confirm the manufacturing leg; a fall would be the first hard-data crack.","signal":"good","signalWord":"m/m rebound held at +2.0%","src":"METI final via Newsquawk and FX.co (16 Nov); preliminary via investingLive and RTTNews","trend":"down","trendWord":"Revised down","url":"https://www.newsquawk.com/headlines/japanese-industrial-production-final-sep-yy-17-prev-27"},{"change":"−0.6 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-0.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.1},{"l":"May 26","v":5.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":0.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.3}],"latest":"+2.3%","name":"Retail Sales, y/y","next":"30 Nov (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+2.9%","read":"September +2.3% y/y, slowing from +2.9% and missing, with household spending −6.8%. October prints 30 Nov at 08:50 with ING and Longbridge expecting +2.7% y/y and +1.1% m/m after −0.6%. Tokyo energy prices rose 9.5% in November as the subsidies ended, so real spending power is the test for the household leg.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Missed; household spending −6.8%","src":"METI via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slowing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/retail-sales-annual"},{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.5},{"l":"May 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.4}],"latest":"2.4%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"1 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"2.5%","read":"September 2.4%, back to the cycle low from 2.5%. October prints 1 Dec at 08:30 (the DZH schedule), not 30 Nov. The labour market is tight and the leg of the virtuous cycle that is not in question.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Tight","src":"MIC via FX.co; history FRED","trend":"down","trendWord":"Back to the low","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3259063"},{"change":"+0.01","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.19},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.19},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.18},{"l":"May 26","v":1.17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.19}],"latest":"1.19","name":"Jobs-to-Applicants Ratio","next":"1 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"1.18","read":"September 1.19, the first rise since June but still under the 1.2 line that the framework treats as tight. October prints 1 Dec at 08:30. A move back above 1.20 would confirm the labour side of the wage story; a slip under 1.18 would say firms are pulling openings.","ref":1,"refLabel":"1.0 = one opening per applicant","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 1.2 line","src":"MHLW via FX.co","trend":"up","trendWord":"First rise since Jun","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3258824"},{"change":"+3.8 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":6.1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":5.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":16.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":4.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":11.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":14.8},{"l":"May 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":19.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":23.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":19.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":16.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":20.5}],"latest":"+20.5%","name":"Exports, y/y","next":"mid-Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"+16.7%","read":"October exports rose 20.5% y/y to ¥11,768.8bn, the highest monthly value on record and the 14th straight gain, against a +20.0% consensus and September's +16.7% (Trading Economics shows September revised to +16.5%). Export volume rose 5.8%, so the gain is not only price. Semiconductors +49.7% by value, electrical machinery +33.6%, machinery +21.2%, transport equipment +18.0% with motor vehicles +19.9%; by destination the US +23.9% (motor vehicles to the US +37.2%, surplus ¥690.6bn), Asia +22.0%, the EU +22.1%, China +13.3% (chip equipment to China +42.6%, motor vehicles to China −53.3%), the Middle East −15.0%. Imports rose 21.6% to ¥12.25tn against about +25.8% expected, on mineral fuels +36.5% and petroleum +60.5% by value for 0.7% less volume (crude from the US +738% by volume, from the Middle East −16.5%); the deficit was ¥476.5bn against ¥955bn expected, a sixth monthly deficit. External demand is the strongest leg of the economy and the chip chain is its whole engine; the US tariff deal is not showing in autos to the US (+37.2%). Next: November trade in mid-December.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Beat; chips +49.7%, volume +5.8%","src":"MoF trade statistics (provisional) via Xinhua, ActionForex, IndexBox and Trading Economics; Newsquawk (consensus); Zaikei (QUICK consensus)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Record, 14th gain","url":"https://english.news.cn/asiapacific/20261118/a9e3802d03274a27b6508b13f7aa7ff5/c.html"},{"change":"+0.6 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-0.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"+2.2%","name":"Real Wages, y/y","next":"7–8 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+1.6%","read":"September real wages rose 2.2% y/y, a ninth straight gain and above the 1.3% consensus, after August was revised to +1.6% from +1.5%. Nominal cash earnings rose 4.6% to ¥311,409, the fastest since January 1997 and above the 3.8% consensus, an eighth month above 3%; August was revised to 3.9%. Base pay rose 3.7% (3.8% in August), same-sample full-time base pay 3.1% (2.9%) and overtime 3.6%; special payments jumped 32.6%, which the ministry attributes to a statistical quirk, so the headline overstates the trend. The deflator CPI was 2.4% (2.2%). The BoJ's virtuous-cycle test is met on base pay but not yet on spending: September household spending fell 6.8% y/y (10 Nov), so the income gain is being saved or lost to weather. The Summary of Opinions has one member expecting next year's wage growth to be consistent with 2% and another expecting little change from this year. October wages print 7–8 Dec.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = wages keep pace with prices","signal":"good","signalWord":"Positive 9 months","src":"MHLW via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex and Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Faster growth","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/japans-real-wages-rise-for-ninth-month-in-september-backing-boj-rate-hike-5006105"},{"change":"−1.5 pt","hist":[{"l":"Dec 23","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 24","v":-0.4},{"l":"Jun 24","v":-0.5},{"l":"Sep 24","v":3.4},{"l":"Dec 24","v":1.6},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2},{"l":"Jun 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Sep 25","v":-1.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.1},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-0.1}],"latest":"−0.1%","name":"Real GDP, q/q annualized","next":"8 Dec (Q3 2nd prelim)","period":"Q3 2026 (1st prelim)","prior":"+1.4%","read":"Real GDP fell 0.02% q/q and 0.1% annualized in July to September (first preliminary, 16 Nov), against a +0.1% q/q and about +0.3% annualized consensus (range −0.3% to +1.5%), the first contraction in four quarters; Q2 was revised to +0.4% q/q and +1.4% annualized. Private consumption fell 0.3% q/q for a second quarter (consensus −0.2%) on food and household goods, weather hits to clothing and lodging, and payback from pre-tax-change purchases of air conditioners and cars; housing −1.1%, government consumption −0.0% (first negative in six quarters), public investment −1.1%, exports −0.2% with R&D services down, imports +2.5% (crude re-sourced after Hormuz), so net exports took 0.6 pt while domestic demand added 0.6 pt. Business investment rose 1.6% q/q (+6.8% annualized, consensus +1.5%) on AI servers and chip equipment, the first rise in three quarters. Nominal GDP +0.4% q/q and +1.6% annualized; deflator +2.4% y/y. Analysts (Meiji Yasuda, Daiwa) read the consumption weakness as price-driven and no bar to a December hike; Mizuho's Katagi says the board split sharpens if it persists. The second estimate is 8 Dec, after the MoF corporate survey on capex.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no growth","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Missed; consumption −0.3% q/q, capex +1.6%","src":"Cabinet Office ESRI first preliminary (16 Nov) via Admiral Markets, Seoul Economic Daily and Yahoo Finance; Mace News consensus","trend":"down","trendWord":"First fall in 4 quarters","url":"https://admiralmarkets.com/analytics/traders-blog/japan-gdp-q3-2026-yen-usdjpy-160"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.4},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"2.2%","name":"Core CPI (ex fresh food), y/y","next":"18 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.1%","read":"October core CPI rose 2.2% from 2.1%, under the 2.3% consensus (Reuters poll of 16; QUICK median 2.3%) and the highest since December 2025, a second month at or above the 2% target. Headline was 2.3% (September 2.1%); food inflation slowed to 2.2% from 2.9% while goods rose 3.2% (2.8%) and services 1.4%, so the mix is energy and goods rather than demand. With September real wages at +2.2% the reading is at target rather than squeezing consumption, and it leaves December intact: Minami (Norinchukin) expects a hike next month and another in March 2027 when inflation is likely to exceed 3%; Shinke (Dai-ichi Life) says only market instability could make the Bank stand pat. Tokyo core for November printed 2.9% on 27 Nov (energy +9.5% after the subsidies ended, core-core 2.5%), so the national November core due 18 Dec at 08:30, the morning of the BoJ decision, is likely to print above 2.2% on energy; consensus is not yet published.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"At target; missed 2.3%","src":"MIC via Reuters (Investing.com, AOL), Trading Economics, ChainCatcher (headline), Fisco via Zaikei (QUICK median)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Highest since Dec 2025","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"−0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"2.2%","name":"Core-core CPI (ex food and energy), y/y","next":"18 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.4%","read":"Core-core CPI eased to 2.2% from September's 2.4%, a six-month high, so the demand-driven measure the BoJ watches is back at target. Goods prices rose 3.2% y/y (2.8% in September) and services 1.4% (1.5%), so the acceleration is in goods, food ex fresh slowed to 2.2% from 2.9%, and services inflation is still short of 2%. Tokyo core-core eased to 2.5% in November from 2.6% in October, so the demand measure is cooling in the early read too. The print trims the overshoot case Ueda made on 19 Nov (underlying inflation 'could run above 2%') without removing December; the pace argument for 2027 is what it bears on.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2%","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"At target, services 1.4%","src":"MIC via Reuters (Investing.com, AOL), Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Eased from 6-month high","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"+0.3 pt","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.5},{"l":"May 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Nov 26","v":2.9}],"latest":"2.9%","name":"Tokyo Core CPI, y/y","next":"late Dec (Dec)","period":"Nov 2026","prior":"2.6%","read":"November core rose to 2.9% from 2.6%, the highest since July 2025 and a third month above 2%, against a 3.0% consensus (Reuters and QUICK medians; OANDA 2.9%), while headline rose to 2.8% and ex fresh food and energy eased to 2.5% from 2.6%. The driver is energy: +9.5% y/y from +2.2% as electricity (+9.5%, +14.2% m/m) and city gas (+13.7%, +13.0% m/m) lost the subsidy that still covered September usage in the October index, adding about 0.34 pt to the change in core; gasoline −1.1%, fresh food 1.8% from 8.6%, food ex fresh 3.1% (3.0%), eating out 4.7%, services 2.1% (2.2%), accommodation 3.6% (6.7%), rent 1.9%, non-Koshihikari rice −33.8%; core m/m +0.5%, core-core +0.1%. Above target on every measure, but the underlying pace cooled for a second month, which is why the yen and futures did not move (USD/JPY 159.26 to 159.28 in the minute after the release; JGB futures up to 128.35). National November CPI prints 18 Dec at 08:30, the morning of the BoJ decision; Middle East energy costs are the next upside input.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Energy-led; core-core eased","src":"MIC via OANDA Japan, investingLive, ActionForex and Trading Economics (27 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Highest since Jul 2025","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/tokyocpi-nov-2026-review/"},{"change":"0 bp (8–1)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":0.75},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.75},{"l":"May 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.25},{"l":"Oct 26","v":1.25},{"l":"27 Nov","v":1.25}],"latest":"1.25%","name":"BoJ Policy Rate","next":"18 Dec","period":"Held 30 Oct; 2-year 1.760% on 27 Nov","prior":"1.25%","read":"Held at 1.25% on 30 Oct by 8–1 with Takata for 1.50%. Tamura (26 Nov) put 50 bp on the table and said a hike will be discussed in December or later; the Tokyo core CPI printed 2.9% against 3.0% on 27 Nov. MogWatch OIS has 18 Dec at 74.7% on trades to 21:06 JST on 27 Nov (unchanged on the day; 84.7% on 20 Nov), implied 1.437%, March 1.595%; the 2-year closed 1.760% (+3 bp), the highest since 24 Nov. Ueda in Nagoya on 30 Nov is the last scheduled word before the 18 Dec meeting.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Tightening; Dec pricing about 75%","src":"Bank of Japan (schedule, speeches page); Reuters via MarketScreener and Jiji via nippon.com (Tamura press conference); MogWatch (OIS 27 Nov 00:10 JST); QUICK/NQN via SMTB (2-year, BoJ operation); OANDA Japan and investingLive (Tokyo CPI); Reuters via Investing.com (poll)","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Hawkish hold","url":"https://mogwatch.jp/boj"},{"change":"+¥1.2tn in Oct","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":697.9},{"l":"Dec 25","v":677.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":682.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":683.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":663},{"l":"Apr 26","v":663.3},{"l":"May 26","v":664.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":639.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":644.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":644.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":625},{"l":"Oct 26","v":626.2}],"latest":"¥626tn","name":"BoJ Total Assets","next":"early Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"¥625tn","read":"Total assets were ¥626.2tn at 31 Oct (BoJ accounts, published 4 Nov), up ¥1.2tn from ¥625.0tn at 30 Sep, with JGB holdings ¥505.3tn (+¥1.3tn): purchases of about ¥2.3tn a month this quarter outran light October redemptions. The balance sheet is down from a ¥765tn peak in Aug 2024 as JGB purchases are cut by about ¥200bn a quarter, heading to ¥2tn a month from April 2027; the June plan targets JGB holdings of about ¥480tn by March 2027. Quarter-end months dip on redemptions (September −¥20tn), so the October uptick is the between-quarter pattern, not a change in policy; ETF sales of about ¥860bn a year start in January.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"QT by taper","src":"Bank of Japan accounts (31 Oct and 30 Sep 2026); BoJ purchase plan","trend":"up","trendWord":"Flat after Q3 dip","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/statistics/boj/other/acmai/release/2026/ac261031.htm"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the leading and coincident core. They are not part of the original indicator list.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"+1.0 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.06},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.24},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.11},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.345},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.515},{"l":"May 26","v":2.65},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.67},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.79},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.94},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.063},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.77},{"l":"27 Nov","v":2.64}],"latest":"2.640%","name":"10-Year JGB Yield","period":"27 Nov close","prior":"2.630% on 26 Nov (QUICK/NQN revised; 2.645% recorded)","read":"The 10-year closed 2.640% on 27 Nov (QUICK/NQN, +1 bp on a revised 2.630% for 26 Nov; the SMTB table shows +0.5 bp; Fisco 2.632%) after a 2.625% morning low on the Tokyo CPI miss, with the Nikkei up 2.11%, US 10-year near 4.92% in Tokyo hours and squaring before the 2-year auction; December futures 128.12 (−12 sen). The MoF reference yield was 2.704% on 26 Nov. The haven bid of 25 Nov has faded and the yield sits between the 2.60% area and the 2.77% October close; the 10-year auction on 3 Dec is the next supply test.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Range-bound","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (2.645%, +2.5 bp, high 2.660%, curve, futures, BoJ operation); SMTB market table (2.635%); Fisco via Zaikei 879296 (2.615%, futures range); QUICK/NQN via SMTB 27 Nov 09:06 (futures open)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Equity-led","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"change":"+3.0 bp","hist":[{"l":"10 Nov","v":1.761},{"l":"11 Nov","v":1.855},{"l":"13 Nov","v":1.88},{"l":"16 Nov","v":1.87},{"l":"17 Nov","v":1.87},{"l":"18 Nov","v":1.86},{"l":"19 Nov","v":1.845},{"l":"20 Nov","v":1.82},{"l":"24 Nov","v":1.765},{"l":"25 Nov","v":1.72},{"l":"26 Nov","v":1.74},{"l":"27 Nov","v":1.76}],"latest":"1.760%","name":"2-Year JGB Yield","period":"27 Nov close","prior":"1.730% on 26 Nov (QUICK/NQN revised; 1.740% recorded)","read":"The 2-year closed 1.760% on 27 Nov (QUICK/NQN; +3 bp on QUICK's revised 1.730% for 26 Nov; Fisco 1.745% earlier in the day), the highest since 24 Nov, on position-squaring before the ¥2.8tn 2-year auction on 30 Nov, the 2.11% equity gain and higher US yields; the MoF reference was 1.747% on 26 Nov. At 1.760% the market prices a December hike to 1.50% as the base case with part of a hold still in (MogWatch 74.7%, implied 1.437%; March 1.595%). A close above 1.85% prices the 2027 pace; under 1.70% says the hold is the base case. Ueda in Nagoya on 30 Nov and the auction tail are the next tells.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Dec hike the base case","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (1.740%, +1.5 bp); Fisco via Zaikei 879296 (1.726%); MogWatch (OIS 27 Nov 00:10 JST); Reuters via MarketScreener (Tamura); OANDA Japan (Tokyo CPI reaction)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Pre-auction","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"change":"−2.0 bp","hist":[{"l":"10 Nov","v":3.865},{"l":"11 Nov","v":3.94},{"l":"13 Nov","v":4},{"l":"16 Nov","v":4.03},{"l":"17 Nov","v":4.04},{"l":"18 Nov","v":4.045},{"l":"19 Nov","v":3.99},{"l":"20 Nov","v":3.975},{"l":"24 Nov","v":3.94},{"l":"25 Nov","v":3.95},{"l":"26 Nov","v":3.895},{"l":"27 Nov","v":3.87}],"latest":"3.870%","name":"30-Year JGB Yield","period":"27 Nov close","prior":"3.890% on 26 Nov (QUICK/NQN revised; 3.895% recorded)","read":"The 30-year closed 3.870% on 27 Nov (QUICK/NQN, −2 bp on a revised 3.890% for 26 Nov) and the 40-year 4.090% (−2.5 bp), a third day lower after the BoJ operation on 26 Nov and the QUICK readout that primary dealers want super-long issuance held flat with any FY2027 increase in other maturities; the MoF reference was 3.956% on 26 Nov. The liquidity-supply auction (5 to 11 years) drew 3.89 cover with a 0.1 bp tail. Under the framework the 30-year is now under the 3.90% half of the pair-off condition but USD/JPY at 159.56 is not under 158.5, so the warn pair (super-long yields with a weak yen) stays on; 4.05% while equities fall is the term-premium signal, 4.30% the fiscal line, and the 30-year auction on 8 Dec the demand test.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Under 3.90%; pair still on","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (30-year 3.895%, 40-year 4.115%, 20-year 3.560%, BoJ operation cover, 25 Nov auction); QUICK Money World headline (PD meeting); Reuters via MarketScreener and FixedIncomeNews (floating-rate bond); MUFG weekly (30-year auction date)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Easing","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"change":"+2.11%","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":50339.48},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53322.85},{"l":"Feb 26","v":58850.27},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51063.72},{"l":"Apr 26","v":59284.92},{"l":"May 26","v":66329.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":70062.32},{"l":"Jul 26","v":64362.02},{"l":"Aug 26","v":66311.93},{"l":"Sep 26","v":66753.72},{"l":"Oct 26","v":63290.11},{"l":"27 Nov","v":58711.11}],"latest":"58,711.11","name":"Nikkei 225","period":"27 Nov close","prior":"57,499.16 on 26 Nov","read":"Closed 58,711.11 (+1,211.95, +2.11%) on 27 Nov near the 58,738.02 high, the first close above the 5-day average since 13 Nov, with 926 Prime names up against 582, 26 of 33 sectors up and the Nikkei VI at 28.91; Advantest and Tokyo Electron added about 555 points and the top five about 738. Wall Street's half-session then added 1.6% to 2.3% with the SOX +4.69%. The drawdown is 19.4% from the 72,831.73 record and the close is 2,274 points above the 20 Nov liquidation close. Under the framework the posture stays Risk-off until a close above 60,030 with TOPIX above 3,895 and the VI under 35; the close is 2.2% short and the VI condition is met.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Risk-off; 2.2% under the line","src":"Kabutan n202611260984 (close, open, high, low, TOPIX, breadth, sectors, volume, Growth 250); Fisco via Zaikei 879302 (contributors) and 879295 (close); Gentosha Gold Online (turnover, movers); OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045983 (futures); Fisco via Investing.com 1771569 (Nikkei VI); Nikkei headline (night futures); OANDA Japan mn_1046143, Fisco via Zaikei 879444 and Reuters via Newsweek Japan (27 Nov morning)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Broad rebound","url":"https://s.kabutan.jp/news/n202611260984/"},{"change":"+1.73%","hist":[{"l":"Dec 24","v":2784.92},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2658.73},{"l":"Jun 25","v":2852.84},{"l":"Sep 25","v":3137.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3408.97},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3497.86},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3994},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4108.65},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3965.07},{"l":"27 Nov","v":3815.2}],"latest":"3,815.20","name":"TOPIX","period":"27 Nov close","prior":"3,750.40 on 26 Nov","read":"Closed 3,815.20 (+64.80, +1.73%) on 27 Nov with 59.7% of Prime advancing and 26 of 33 sectors up, led by nonferrous metals, glass, oil and coal, electrical equipment and machinery, against services, land transport and retail. The index is 2.1% under the 3,895 first-test line and 3.8% above the 20 Nov low of 3,674.22. Two broad up-sessions in a row after a week of four-name moves is the best breadth read of the drawdown; the posture stays Risk-off until the lines are retaken on a close.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Risk-off; 2.1% under 3,895","src":"Kabutan n202611260984 and Fisco via Zaikei 879295 (26 Nov close, breadth, sectors); OANDA Japan mn_1046143 and Reuters via Newsweek Japan (27 Nov morning); kabutore.biz quarterly closes","trend":"up","trendWord":"Broad gain","url":"https://s.kabutan.jp/news/n202611260984/"},{"change":"+0.31","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":156.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":154.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":155.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":159.8},{"l":"Apr 26","v":160.2},{"l":"May 26","v":159.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":161.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":160.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":160.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":157.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":157.55},{"l":"27 Nov","v":159.56}],"latest":"159.56","name":"USD/JPY","period":"27 Nov (New York close)","prior":"159.25 on 26 Nov","read":"Closed 159.56 in New York on 27 Nov (DZH; +0.31; Fisco gives 159.60) after a 159.43 low and a 159.60 high on London-fix dollar buying at 01:00 JST, the month-end fix, with US 2-year 4.69% (+8 bp) and 10-year 4.94% (+9 bp) and the dollar index 104.04; 159.36 in Tokyo at 17:09. No MoF or Katayama comment was found; Gaitame's weekly puts the upside pivot at 159.60 and the 13 Nov high at 160.18, with 158.40 below. Under the framework the pair is 44 sen from 160 and the intervention zone with the front-end gap about 293 bp; the November intervention total prints 30 Nov at 19:00 and Ueda speaks at 10:05.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Intervention zone","src":"Gaitame (159.25 close, 159.34 high, 27 Nov outlook); Myforex DZH dzh261127015 and dzh261127028 (New York 2:00 and 4:00 JST, range 158.95 to 159.34, EUR/JPY, EUR/USD); OANDA Japan mn_1045961 (Tokyo 15:00), tokyocpi-nov-2026-review (08:30 reaction) and mn_1046144 (27 Nov noon)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Toward 160","url":"https://www.gaitame.com/media/entry/2026/11/27/101539"}],"sub":"Additions to the leading and coincident core. Closing levels for Wednesday 4 Nov unless a date is shown; 5 Nov figures are intraday.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["Leading indicators: no new print. The November flash PMIs stand: manufacturing 53.7 (October 53.3, consensus 53.5), output 53.8, export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018; services 50.5 (51.7) with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5, a six-month low. Standing: BoJ Tankan +24, Reuters Tankan +27 and +35, core machinery orders +13.4% y/y, consumer confidence 35.0, housing starts −2.7% (October prints 30 Nov at 14:00; Longbridge expects −6.7% y/y). The September leading index was revised to 116.5 and the coincident index held at 119.5 (26 Nov). The manufacturing final is 1 Dec, consumer confidence 2 Dec, the services final 3 Dec; the December Tankan is 15 Dec.","Coincident data: no new print. October exports +20.5% y/y to a record ¥11,768.8bn; Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized (consumption −0.3% q/q, capex +1.6%; second estimate 8 Dec); industrial production +1.7% y/y (+2.0% m/m); real wages +2.2% (nominal +4.6%); unemployment 2.4%; jobs ratio 1.19; retail sales +2.3%. The October hard data start 30 Nov at 08:50: industrial production (ING and Longbridge expect +2.1% m/m and +2.7% y/y; MetaTrader's calendar shows +0.2% m/m; the METI survey had +5.1% m/m), retail sales (+2.7% y/y expected from a revised +2.4%), then housing starts at 14:00 and the November intervention total at 19:00; unemployment, the jobs ratio and the MoF corporate survey (Q3 capex expected +4.3% y/y) print 1 Dec at 08:30 and 08:50. The Bank of Korea raised its rate to 3.25% on 26 Nov (5–2), the highest since November 2024, and lifted its 2026 growth forecast to 3.9% and 2027 to 4.1% on the chip cycle, the same demand that drives Japan's export orders. With core CPI at 2.2% and real wages at +2.2% the virtuous-cycle test is still passed.","Prices and policy: Tokyo November CPI (27 Nov): core 2.9% y/y from 2.6%, the highest since July 2025, against a 3.0% consensus; headline 2.8%; core-core 2.5% from 2.6%; energy +9.5% (electricity +9.5%, city gas +13.7%) as the subsidies ended, food ex fresh 3.1%, services 2.1%; core m/m +0.5%. Tamura (26 Nov) put 50 bp on the table with 25 bp every few months as his base case and December \"or a later meeting\" as the venue. MogWatch OIS: 74.7% for 18 Dec on trades to 21:06 JST on 27 Nov, unchanged through the CPI (73.5% at 13:07 JST per NERO; 76.7% on 26 Nov, 84.7% on 20 Nov), implied 1.437%, January 1.484%, March 1.595% (1.50% the modal March outcome at 45.4%, 1.75% or higher 43.1%), average change +18.7 bp; Goldman Sachs expects December, March and June hikes to 2.0%; Nomura says the December chance \"remains high\" and sees USD/JPY at 154 in one month; ANZ calls aggressive tightening unwarranted. JGBs 27 Nov (QUICK/NQN, 15:28): 2-year 1.760% (+3 bp on a revised 1.730%), 5-year 2.065% (+1.5 bp), 10-year 2.640% (+1 bp; SMTB table +0.5 bp; morning low 2.625%), 20-year 3.560% (+0.5 bp), 30-year 3.870% (−2 bp), 40-year 4.090% (−2.5 bp), December futures 128.12 (−12 sen; 128.09 to 128.41), TONA December 98.5625 unchanged; the ¥500bn liquidity-supply auction (5 to 11 years) drew 3.89 cover (3.77) with a 0.1 bp tail, somewhat strong; drivers were the 2.11% equity gain, US 10-year near 4.92% in Tokyo hours, WTI in the mid-$85s in the morning and squaring before the 2-year auction. The MoF primary-dealer meeting (26 Nov, QUICK readout): participants said super-long issuance should stay flat after the past cuts, some said certain tenors could be cut further, and any increase would fall in other maturities, which the market read as supportive; the investor-meeting readout (27 Nov) was not found. The MoF December calendar: 10-year 3 Dec, 30-year 8 Dec, 5-year 10 Dec, liquidity supply 15 and 22 Dec, 20-year 17 Dec, 2-year 24 Dec. The Fed: CME 97.4% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec (Polymarket 94.5%); US 2-year 4.69% (+8 bp), 10-year 4.94% (+9 bp), 30-year 5.33% (+9 bp) on 27 Nov.","Yen and oil: USD/JPY closed 27 Nov in New York at 159.56 (DZH; +0.31 from 159.25; Fisco gives the close as 159.60), after a 159.43 low and a 159.60 high on London-fix dollar buying at 01:00 JST, the month-end fix; 159.36 in Tokyo at 17:09 (159.22 at 07:17) with the Nikkei up 2.1%; EUR/JPY 174.36 (−0.34) and EUR/USD 1.0936 (−0.0033) after soft French and Spanish flash CPI and an S&P negative outlook on France; dollar index 104.04 (+0.36%). Drivers: US long yields up 8 to 9 bp after the holiday on a +0.9% wholesale-inventories print, a wider October trade deficit (−$149.5bn) and higher oil on the Jazan refinery attack. No MoF or Katayama comment was found; Fisco's afternoon note cited caution over coordinated US-Japan intervention near 160, Gaitame's weekly sets 159.60 as the upside pivot, 160.18 (13 Nov high) above it and 158.40 below, with a 157.30 to 160.30 range for the week; the November intervention total prints 30 Nov at 19:00. Oil: WTI January settled $83.08 (−0.72%, a fourth weekly loss) and Brent January $95.44 (+0.88%) after a projectile hit Saudi Aramco's Jazan refinery, with Hormuz flows at 31% of pre-war levels per Kpler; OPEC+ is reported to have agreed in principle to hold targets through Q1 2027 ahead of its 29 Nov meeting; gold $3,668.50 (+3.75%).","Markets: Tokyo 27 Nov close: Nikkei 58,711.11 (+1,211.95, +2.11%), open 57,669.79, high 58,738.02, low 57,447.16, the first close above the 5-day average since 13 Nov; TOPIX 3,815.20 (+64.80, +1.73%); Prime 926 up, 582 down (59.7% advancing), 26 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, glass, oil and coal, electrical equipment, machinery) against services, land transport, retail, other products and real estate; turnover ¥7,787.2bn on 2,401.89m shares; contributors: Advantest 26,585 (+1,755, +379 points), Tokyo Electron 8,220 (+349, +176), SoftBank Group 2,984.5 (+90.5, +73), Ibiden 5,120 (+452, +61), TDK 2,306 (+49), Kioxia 8,450 (+688, +49), Fanuc 5,339 (+41), Lasertec 33,600 (+3,050, +41), Toyota Tsusho +27, Fujikura +25, Murata +21; Recruit 16,700 (−545, −55), Konami 30,470 (−720, −24), Fast Retailing 85,330 (−170, −14), Terumo −12; 160 constituents up, 64 down; the top five added about 738 points and the bottom five took about 115. Nikkei VI 28.91 (−2.22, −7.13%) after 42.35 intraday; Growth 250 680.38 (+27.78); futures 58,817.50 (+930) on an untimed investing.com snapshot. Kabutan cites European strength, Nasdaq 100 futures, Korean stocks, calm JGBs and dividend reinvestment; Kioxia rose on a second TOPIX float-ratio increase, JT on a reported bid, Mitsubishi Heavy on a report of ¥56.3tn defence spending. Wall Street 27 Nov (1 pm close): S&P 500 7,297.49 (+114.71, +1.60%), Nasdaq 24,129.50 (+543.70, +2.31%), Dow 50,774.94 (+591.99, +1.18%), Russell 2000 2,750.30 (+1.71%), VIX 23.81 (−15.8%), SOX +4.69%, advancers 406 to 94; Nvidia +0.80% to $150.49 after a dip on the FT report of halted Southeast Asian shipments and a Bloomberg report of Blackwell chips installed in Shanghai, AMD +4.56%, Micron +7.60%, Broadcom +3.82%, Sandisk +15.96%, Dell +6.70%, Anthropic $91.88 (+10.98%, above the $83 IPO price); weekly S&P 500 +5.60%, the best since April 2025, Dow +5.46%, Nasdaq +6.68%. The first stabilisation test (60,030, 3,895, VI under 35) is 2.2% and 2.1% away at the close with the VI condition met; a close under 56,436.65 is a new low."],"translation":[{"body":"The 27 Nov close (Nikkei 58,711.11, +2.11%; TOPIX 3,815.20, +1.73%) was the second broad up-session of the drawdown: 926 Prime names rose against 582, 26 of 33 sectors gained, and the index still took most of its points from Advantest (+379) and Tokyo Electron (+176), with Kioxia, Ibiden, TDK, Fanuc and Lasertec behind them and Recruit, Konami and Fast Retailing the drags. The Nikkei VI closed 28.91 after a 42.35 intraday spike, the second close under 35, and the index closed above its 5-day average for the first time since 13 Nov while the 25-day and 13-week lines still fall. Wall Street's half-session added 1.6% to 2.3% with the SOX +4.69% and the VIX at 23.81, so the 30 Nov open has a tailwind. Under the framework the drawdown is 19.4% from the record, the 20 Nov liquidation close (56,436.65) is 2,274 points below, and the posture stays Risk-off until a close retakes 60,030 and 3,895, which the close misses by 2.2% and 2.1% with the VI condition met. The Section 301 risk is unchanged after Kihara's protest; the Huang testimony (1 and 3 Dec) and Ueda (30 Nov) are the next inputs for the AI leaders and the yen.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"JGBs sold at the front end and belly on 27 Nov: the 2-year rose 3 bp to 1.760% (highest since 24 Nov), the 5-year 1.5 bp to 2.065% and the 10-year 1 bp to 2.640% after a 2.625% morning low on the Tokyo CPI miss, on the 2.11% equity gain, US 10-year near 4.92% in Tokyo hours and squaring before the 30 Nov 2-year auction; the 20-year was flat at 3.560% and the 30-year and 40-year eased 2 and 2.5 bp to 3.870% and 4.090% after the QUICK readout that primary dealers want super-long issuance held flat; the liquidity-supply auction drew 3.89 cover with a 0.1 bp tail; futures closed 128.12 (−12 sen). The read under the framework: the 2-year at 1.760% prices the December hike as the base case with part of a hold still in (MogWatch 74.7%, implied 1.437%, March 1.595%), the super-long end is under the 3.90% half of the pair-off condition on supply expectations rather than demand evidence, and the warn pair stays on because USD/JPY closed 159.56, not under 158.5. The yen traded the US curve, not Japan: US 2-year and 10-year yields rose 8 and 9 bp after the holiday and the pair made its 159.60 high at the month-end London fix; the front-end gap is about 293 bp, 160 is 44 sen away, no MoF comment has been found since 19 Nov, and the November intervention total prints 30 Nov.","title":"Fixed income and currency"},{"body":"The BoJ is tightening into an equity drawdown of 19.4% from the record with the early inflation read above target (Tokyo core 2.9%, core-core 2.5%; national core 2.2%; services PPI +4.2%), real wages positive (+2.2%) and the composite PMI at a six-month low (51.5). Tamura wants the rate at neutral (about 2%) soon, 25 bp every few months as the base case and 50 bp if prices overshoot; the market prices 74.7% for December and 1.595% by March, unchanged through the Tokyo CPI; Goldman expects three hikes to 2.0% by June, ANZ calls aggressive tightening unwarranted, and Ueda speaks in Nagoya on 30 Nov. The Fed is at 97.4% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec with US yields up 8 to 9 bp on 27 Nov, so a BoJ hike to 1.50% leaves the gap near 293 bp and the pace difference is what the yen trades. The MoF side: primary dealers asked for super-long issuance to stay flat with any FY2027 increase in shorter maturities (QUICK), the investor-meeting readout is not yet public, the debut 2-year floater is shelved, the plan is due at year-end, and the December calendar runs 10-year 3 Dec, 30-year 8 Dec, 5-year 10 Dec, 20-year 17 Dec, 2-year 24 Dec with liquidity-supply auctions 15 and 22 Dec; the food consumption-tax cut (about ¥5tn a year, unfunded) is in the Upper House to 12 Dec. The Bank of Korea's hike to 3.25% (26 Nov, 5–2) with a 4.1% 2027 growth forecast is the regional read on the chip cycle. The USTR Section 301 proposal is a political item after Kihara's protest. The balance sheet (¥626.2tn) is unchanged; the taper continues toward ¥2tn a month of JGB purchases from April 2027.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
